INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "SEC complaint, DOJ case page, CNBC report, and Guardian investigation", "source_url": "SRC-004; SRC-002; SRC-001; SRC-003", "text": "Evidence summary: SEC complaint alleges HyperFund was a global crypto-asset multi-level-marketing pyramid and Ponzi scheme raising over $1.7 billion, operated through projects collectively called HyperFund. HyperTech Group launched HyperFund in June 2020; it later rebranded as HyperVerse around December 2021 and HyperNation around May 2022, and is now defunct. Membership packages promised 0.5%-1% daily passive returns and potentially tripling investment in 600 days, plus referral-based accelerated rewards. SEC alleges there was no legitimate revenue or large-scale mining; withdrawals were paid from new investor deposits. It identifies Xue Samuel Lee/Sam Lee as co-founder and Brenda Indah Chunga/Bitcoin Beautee as a top promoter who received over $3.7m and used wealth displays to recruit. SEC alleges an actor was hired to pretend to be HyperVerse CEO. SEC complaint also describes memberships bought with USDT, internal HyperUnits, and withdrawal conversion through MOF/HOO Exchange. DOJ page says Lee was charged with conspiracy to commit securities and wire fraud; Chunga pleaded guilty to the same conspiracy charge; Rodney Burton/Bitcoin Rodney was a promoter and was charged in money-transmission counts. DOJ stresses charges are allegations and defendants are presumed innocent. DOJ alleges $1.89b fraud, false mining claims, and blocked withdrawals beginning at least July 2021. CNBC reports DOJ announced a $1.9b alleged worldwide Ponzi fraud; Lee and promoters Burton and Chunga charged, Chunga pleaded guilty, and SEC brought related civil action. Guardian reports the alleged CEO Steven Reece Lewis was presented with claimed Leeds/Cambridge, Goldman Sachs, Adobe and startup credentials that Guardian could not verify; no records were found at the universities, UK/US company or SEC records, Adobe filings, or Goldman Sachs. Guardian reports celebrity promotional messages from Steve Wozniak, Chuck Norris, Jim Norton and Lance Bass, with no suggestion celebrities knew the business model; messages were potentially Cameo-style paid marketing. Guardian describes $300 minimum memberships, 0.5% daily minimum returns, 300% over 600 days, reinvestment incentives, and referral commissions down 20 levels. Guardian reports members had withdrawal problems and were encouraged to migrate to HyperNation; a promoter called the entities one big entity, while Lee later denied involvement in HyperNation. Lee denied HyperVerse was a scam and disputed being its founder."}
